WEBVTT

NOTE Episode: The Plunk That Answered Back

1
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:25.920
<v Old Timber>There is our song. Come near the pond, little ones, and bring your best listening face. Mine is mostly ears, with a little eyebrow for balance.</v>

2
00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:35.731
<v Old Timber>Have you ever heard a sound that made you stop because you could not see what made it? Not a frightening sound. Just a small sound with a question tucked inside.</v>

3
00:00:36.081 --> 00:00:48.902
<v Old Timber>Sometimes a hidden thing is not trouble at all. Sometimes it is an invitation to look, listen, and search carefully. Could searching honestly help us honor the truth in what God made?</v>

4
00:00:49.252 --> 00:00:58.874
<v Old Timber>The morning I remember began after a long rain, when every leaf along the creek bank held one bright drop and the creek had three different answers waiting.</v>

5
00:01:00.563 --> 00:01:07.483
<v Old Timber>Rex the young river otter came sliding down the wet bank, bright-eyed and ready to love the first thing he met.</v>

6
00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:14.461
<v Rex>Wet mud! New favorite thing! It is a path, a slide, and a surprise for your elbows.</v>

7
00:01:14.811 --> 00:01:21.171
<v Old Timber>A drop slipped from the lowest leaf-tip and struck the longest of three equal-width hollow reeds.</v>

8
00:01:21.521 --> 00:01:23.801
<v Rex>Plunk? Hello?</v>

9
00:01:24.151 --> 00:01:27.743
<v Old Timber>A second, matching drop fell onto the middle-length reed.</v>

10
00:01:28.093 --> 00:01:31.653
<v Rex>Oh! Hello to you too. Are there two of you?</v>

11
00:01:32.003 --> 00:01:35.323
<v Old Timber>Then a third matching drop tapped the shortest reed.</v>

12
00:01:35.673 --> 00:01:43.286
<v Rex>Three! I have discovered a very small committee. I will ask a careful question. Plunk?</v>

13
00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:51.926
<v Old Timber>The reeds said nothing. Rex leaned closer and arranged his whiskers into what he believed was an official listening face.</v>

14
00:01:52.276 --> 00:01:57.356
<v Rex>I can wait. I am excellent at waiting for nearly four seconds.</v>

15
00:01:57.706 --> 00:02:02.626
<v Old Timber>At nearly three seconds, a small bindle rustled out from under a broad dock leaf.</v>

16
00:02:02.976 --> 00:02:08.486
<v Bramble>Morning. Are we waiting for the reeds, or have the reeds been waiting for us?</v>

17
00:02:08.836 --> 00:02:13.832
<v Old Timber>That was Bramble, the traveling hedgehog, his little bindle tucked beside him.</v>

18
00:02:14.182 --> 00:02:22.882
<v Rex>They answered me three times. Low plunk, middle plunk, tiny high plunk. Then they became private.</v>

19
00:02:23.232 --> 00:02:25.847
<v Bramble>What happened just before each answer?</v>

20
00:02:26.197 --> 00:02:34.235
<v Rex>I said hello before the first one. Then I looked surprised before the second. Then I looked even more surprised.</v>

21
00:02:34.585 --> 00:02:37.764
<v Bramble>So your first theory is that surprise makes music.</v>

22
00:02:38.114 --> 00:02:42.154
<v Rex>Correct. I will now be surprised in the name of science.</v>

23
00:02:42.504 --> 00:02:46.041
<v Rex>Oh! Ah! A root I did not expect at all!</v>

24
00:02:46.391 --> 00:02:48.021
<v Bramble>The root appears unmoved.</v>

25
00:02:48.371 --> 00:02:56.312
<v Bramble>Look above us. Three fresh drops are creeping toward three leaf-tips. Rex is still watching that very surprising root.</v>

26
00:02:56.662 --> 00:03:02.862
<v Rex>It answered while I was not asking! Bramble, this mystery has manners of its own.</v>

27
00:03:03.565 --> 00:03:12.215
<v Old Timber>Shelly the young tortoise arrived one measured step at a time and stopped where she could see the reeds, the leaves, and both searchers.</v>

28
00:03:12.565 --> 00:03:14.911
<v Shelly>You are both looking at the reeds.</v>

29
00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:23.834
<v Rex>Because the reeds are talking. Possibly in plunk. I only know three words so far, and one may be a greeting.</v>

30
00:03:24.184 --> 00:03:26.304
<v Shelly>The leaves are dripping.</v>

31
00:03:26.654 --> 00:03:34.854
<v Old Timber>Fern the young fawn had followed Shelly through the wet grass. She stood beneath the branch, watching the drops instead of the reeds.</v>

32
00:03:35.204 --> 00:03:43.317
<v Fern>Um... I noticed something. Each plunk came after a drop. Never before it.</v>

33
00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:49.842
<v Rex>Then the drips may be asking the questions. That is much better than my hello theory.</v>

34
00:03:50.192 --> 00:03:55.751
<v Shelly>Let us change one thing at a time. First: could it be an echo?</v>

35
00:03:56.101 --> 00:03:56.771
<v Rex>Plunk!</v>

36
00:03:57.121 --> 00:04:02.589
<v Bramble>Your voice crossed the water and came back as a faint little plunk from the far bank.</v>

37
00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:07.939
<v Rex>Aha! The far bank speaks plunk too. We may need name badges.</v>

38
00:04:08.289 --> 00:04:12.029
<v Shelly>That echo used your voice. The reeds did not.</v>

39
00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:20.577
<v Rex>Right. An echo copies the sound it receives. Our mystery made three notes without copying my excellent plunk.</v>

40
00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:28.037
<v Rex>I am placing echo theory in the maybe-later pile. The pile is made-up, but very well sorted.</v>

41
00:04:28.387 --> 00:04:37.640
<v Old Timber>Garingo the parrot had been listening from a willow bough. He dropped beside the made-up pile with the grave expression of an official witness.</v>

42
00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:47.990
<v Garingo>BEHOLD! I preserved the evidence: low, middle, high. Three notes in that order, each paired with a drip.</v>

43
00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:53.020
<v Fern>The drip came first, Gah-Ring-Goh. That part matters too.</v>

44
00:04:53.370 --> 00:05:02.908
<v Garingo>Revised record! A drip, then a note. Low, middle, high. I shall guard the order with my entire beak.</v>

45
00:05:03.258 --> 00:05:11.698
<v Shelly>Echo theory: useful, but not our answer. Second: something small may be tapping the stems.</v>

46
00:05:12.048 --> 00:05:16.080
<v Rex>I volunteer this pebble. It has a trustworthy roundness.</v>

47
00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:25.990
<v Shelly>The pebble made a dry little tok against the longest reed. That was not the round low plunk we heard before.</v>

48
00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.531
<v Rex>Tok is not plunk. The pebble has failed its interview.</v>

49
00:05:30.881 --> 00:05:32.599
<v Shelly>It answered the question.</v>

50
00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:44.364
<v Rex>A wrong guess can still give a right clue. Tok tells us a hard tap is not enough. We need whatever makes the sound round.</v>

51
00:05:44.714 --> 00:05:49.670
<v Rex>Then it is promoted to Assistant Pebble for Not Being the Answer.</v>

52
00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:56.909
<v Shelly>Third: perhaps moving water strikes all three at once. A small splash, if you please.</v>

53
00:05:57.259 --> 00:06:00.979
<v Rex>I have trained for this moment my entire morning.</v>

54
00:06:01.329 --> 00:06:07.691
<v Bramble>There goes Rex into the shallows. One neat fan of water is heading straight for the reeds.</v>

55
00:06:08.041 --> 00:06:11.790
<v Rex>That was more of a plunk committee falling down the stairs.</v>

56
00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:14.820
<v Shelly>And the real notes came one at a time.</v>

57
00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:23.810
<v Rex>One at a time means one cause at a time. My splash asked three questions together and received a heap instead of an answer.</v>

58
00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:31.845
<v Rex>Next test: smaller, slower, and with no heroic entrance by me. I can do at least two of those.</v>

59
00:06:32.195 --> 00:06:33.656
<v Shelly>I can supply slower.</v>

60
00:06:34.006 --> 00:06:38.874
<v Bramble>I have smaller in my bindle, though it may be under the travel oatcake.</v>

61
00:06:39.224 --> 00:06:43.424
<v Shelly>Keep the oatcake. Mysteries take longer when everyone is hungry.</v>

62
00:06:43.774 --> 00:06:52.271
<v Garingo>The official order survives: low, middle, high. Shelly found the shape, and Fern found what starts it.</v>

63
00:06:52.621 --> 00:06:58.951
<v Rex>God made the rain and the reeds. I wonder if the answer is hiding in how they meet.</v>

64
00:06:59.301 --> 00:07:04.221
<v Fern>Then we should keep the clues we actually have, not the answer we hoped to find.</v>

65
00:07:04.571 --> 00:07:13.731
<v Shelly>Above us are three equal-width reeds of different lengths. Each leaf holds one drop about the same size.</v>

66
00:07:14.081 --> 00:07:20.274
<v Bramble>And the sun has reached the first leaf. We may not have many drops left to test.</v>

67
00:07:20.624 --> 00:07:29.304
<v Rex>Then we need leaf-drip experiment number one. Which is also our first leaf-drip experiment, so the title is accurate.</v>

68
00:07:29.654 --> 00:07:31.979
<v Shelly>Accurate titles save time.</v>

69
00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:39.803
<v Bramble>I will bend this broad leaf very gently. Its waiting drop is now just above the longest reed.</v>

70
00:07:40.153 --> 00:07:51.864
<v Rex>Before it falls, my prediction is low plunk. Not because the reed knows us. Because this is the same leaf and the same long hollow stem.</v>

71
00:07:52.214 --> 00:07:57.254
<v Rex>If it says tok, squeak, or good morning, my idea needs work.</v>

72
00:07:57.604 --> 00:08:01.666
<v Bramble>Same reed. One drop. Everyone listen.</v>

73
00:08:02.016 --> 00:08:04.493
<v Bramble>There it goes, straight into the open top.</v>

74
00:08:04.843 --> 00:08:11.633
<v Rex>Low plunk! The leaf is involved. Assistant Pebble, record that in your round little memory.</v>

75
00:08:11.983 --> 00:08:20.263
<v Rex>One match does not finish it. We need the other reeds. The answer must work when my whiskers are not cheering for it.</v>

76
00:08:20.613 --> 00:08:22.333
<v Shelly>Now the middle leaf.</v>

77
00:08:22.683 --> 00:08:31.525
<v Shelly>The breeze moved the branch. The middle drop is running sideways, down the back of its leaf. It missed the reed.</v>

78
00:08:31.875 --> 00:08:37.195
<v Rex>No! We lost the middle plunk. It had excellent middle qualities.</v>

79
00:08:37.545 --> 00:08:44.321
<v Garingo>The middle note has vanished! The evidence ledger now contains one very dramatic blank.</v>

80
00:08:44.671 --> 00:08:47.671
<v Fern>It is still only one missing drop.</v>

81
00:08:48.021 --> 00:08:51.892
<v Bramble>One missing note leaves two notes and a sharper question.</v>

82
00:08:52.242 --> 00:09:00.362
<v Fern>We can make one careful drop. Same amount of water, same opening, and no branch-shaking.</v>

83
00:09:00.712 --> 00:09:08.462
<v Bramble>Rex has one bead of creek water on his claw. Wait. Another drop is falling into the shortest reed first.</v>

84
00:09:08.812 --> 00:09:14.045
<v Rex>High plunk! Same kind of drop, different reed, different note.</v>

85
00:09:14.395 --> 00:09:22.545
<v Garingo>Low from long, high from short. The missing middle note now has one very specific and important duty.</v>

86
00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:29.495
<v Fern>If length matters, the middle reed should make the middle note. If it does not, we change our answer.</v>

87
00:09:29.845 --> 00:09:34.045
<v Shelly>The reeds are different lengths. They have not been hiding that.</v>

88
00:09:34.395 --> 00:09:38.505
<v Garingo>The middle reed has one job left: make the middle note.</v>

89
00:09:38.855 --> 00:09:42.895
<v Shelly>And if it disagrees, the mystery is not being rude.</v>

90
00:09:43.245 --> 00:09:48.893
<v Bramble>Quite. Evidence has never once said sorry to me, even in the borderlands.</v>

91
00:09:49.243 --> 00:09:52.082
<v Shelly>Perhaps you need a second made-up pile.</v>

92
00:09:52.432 --> 00:09:56.068
<v Rex>Middle reed, you may disagree in a kind way.</v>

93
00:09:56.418 --> 00:10:04.418
<v Old Timber>He placed his little water bead over the middle reed. At that exact moment, the breeze returned and shook the whole branch.</v>

94
00:10:04.768 --> 00:10:06.443
<v Garingo>Save the clues!</v>

95
00:10:06.793 --> 00:10:16.202
<v Old Timber>Rex lunged with both paws. He caught no drops, bumped the branch, and sent the last three beads raining into the mud beside the reeds.</v>

96
00:10:16.552 --> 00:10:20.865
<v Rex>I caught the branch. The branch was not attempting escape.</v>

97
00:10:21.215 --> 00:10:24.055
<v Shelly>And now the leaves are empty.</v>

98
00:10:24.405 --> 00:10:33.885
<v Old Timber>The sun moved across the reed patch. One by one, the wet rims turned dull. Their little mystery had stopped making any sound at all.</v>

99
00:10:34.235 --> 00:10:39.423
<v Rex>We were close. I could feel the answer getting almost-shaped.</v>

100
00:10:39.773 --> 00:10:48.133
<v Rex>I do not want to invent the missing part just because the real drops are gone. That would make a neat story and a poor answer.</v>

101
00:10:48.483 --> 00:10:56.176
<v Rex>We still know the order, the lengths, the open tops, and the sound of a pebble. None of those clues dried up.</v>

102
00:10:56.526 --> 00:11:04.136
<v Fern>And we know every true note came after water reached an open top. Losing the drops did not erase that.</v>

103
00:11:04.848 --> 00:11:07.576
<v Bramble>Then let us stop shaping it for a moment.</v>

104
00:11:07.926 --> 00:11:08.852
<v Rex>All right.</v>

105
00:11:09.202 --> 00:11:10.893
<v Shelly>The reeds are still here.</v>

106
00:11:11.243 --> 00:11:13.283
<v Fern>So are the things we already noticed.</v>

107
00:11:13.633 --> 00:11:15.751
<v Garingo>I can guard the order quietly.</v>

108
00:11:16.101 --> 00:11:23.901
<v Old Timber>They waited. Water crept along a bent twig above the reeds, gathered at its tip, and became one new drop.</v>

109
00:11:24.251 --> 00:11:25.891
<v Old Timber>It fell into the middle reed.</v>

110
00:11:26.241 --> 00:11:29.214
<v Rex>Middle plunk. The prediction held.</v>

111
00:11:29.564 --> 00:11:38.324
<v Fern>Waiting gave us a fresh test that pushing could not make: same kind of drop, middle-length hollow reed, middle note.</v>

112
00:11:38.674 --> 00:11:43.994
<v Rex>But why does hollow matter? The pebble only said tok against the outside.</v>

113
00:11:44.344 --> 00:11:47.264
<v Fern>What can the open top do that the outside cannot?</v>

114
00:11:47.614 --> 00:11:53.664
<v Rex>Air. A long pocket of air. Does the drop make that pocket wobble?</v>

115
00:11:54.014 --> 00:11:57.771
<v Shelly>Use one gentle breath across each opening.</v>

116
00:11:58.121 --> 00:12:04.044
<v Old Timber>Rex blew gently across the longest reed. Its air column answered with the low note.</v>

117
00:12:04.394 --> 00:12:10.094
<v Garingo>Same gentle breath. Middle reed next. The ledger is listening.</v>

118
00:12:10.444 --> 00:12:14.877
<v Rex>Middle note. Now the shortest reed, with the same breath again.</v>

119
00:12:15.227 --> 00:12:23.027
<v Old Timber>The shortest equal-width reed answered highest. Low, middle, high: the same order as the drips.</v>

120
00:12:23.377 --> 00:12:32.260
<v Rex>The drop started each sound, and the hollow length clearly helped shape the note. Longer came out lower and shorter higher!</v>

121
00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:37.682
<v Garingo>The ledger agrees! Three plunks, one honest pattern!</v>

122
00:12:38.032 --> 00:12:47.352
<v Shelly>The equal widths and matching drops kept the comparison fair. That supports the pattern, but it does not prove every wobble inside it.</v>

123
00:12:47.702 --> 00:12:56.575
<v Rex>And the middle note did exactly what the middle note should do. I trust this answer more than my first four answers.</v>

124
00:12:56.925 --> 00:13:00.805
<v Shelly>It also survived Rex thanking it before he understood it.</v>

125
00:13:01.155 --> 00:13:04.240
<v Bramble>A sturdy answer should manage a little courtesy.</v>

126
00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:07.430
<v Shelly>The reeds managed it by saying nothing.</v>

127
00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:17.660
<v Rex>And it is better than talking reeds. Not louder-better. Wonder-better. Ordinary water was carrying three songs in its pockets.</v>

128
00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:22.610
<v Fern>Hidden did not mean magical. It meant we had more to notice.</v>

129
00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:32.920
<v Rex>I like that God made a world where careful questions open into wonder, even when ordinary drops and hollow reeds make the pattern.</v>

130
00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:41.860
<v Old Timber>Rex listened once more. The sun had dried the leaves, so the reeds offered no encore. He bowed to them anyway.</v>

131
00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:48.010
<v Rex>Thank you, plunk committee. Your report was brief, hollow, and very musical.</v>

132
00:13:48.718 --> 00:13:56.998
<v Old Timber>Later, by the pond, I asked one question. Rex, what made your searching careful instead of merely busy?</v>

133
00:13:57.348 --> 00:14:08.103
<v Rex>We noticed what happened before the sound. We changed one thing at a time. We let wrong answers help us, and we stopped long enough to listen.</v>

134
00:14:08.453 --> 00:14:17.893
<v Garingo>And sometimes honoring a grand mystery means letting its answer be small, true, and quieter than your first speech.</v>

135
00:14:18.243 --> 00:14:26.043
<v Shelly>A hidden thing can invite a search. It does not have to be a warning or a secret message meant only for us.</v>

136
00:14:26.393 --> 00:14:35.356
<v Old Timber>Yes. This is one of King Solomon's proverbs in the Bible: Proverbs, chapter twenty-five, verse two, in the King James Version.</v>

137
00:14:35.706 --> 00:14:41.506
<v Old Timber>It is the glory of God to conceal a thing: but the honour of kings is to search out a matter.</v>

138
00:14:41.856 --> 00:14:45.336
<v Rex>Does that mean a king needs an official listening face?</v>

139
00:14:45.686 --> 00:14:54.766
<v Old Timber>The king-language does not call children kings. It says careful searching is good and worthy work, the sort a wise ruler ought to do.</v>

140
00:14:55.116 --> 00:14:57.956
<v Old Timber>And wonder need not hurry past either one.</v>

141
00:14:58.658 --> 00:15:04.858
<v Old Timber>Professor Hoot the owl swept onto a low branch with one completely true fact about careful listening.</v>

142
00:15:05.208 --> 00:15:08.588
<v Professor Hoot>Many owl species have asymmetrical ear openings.</v>

143
00:15:08.938 --> 00:15:18.759
<v Professor Hoot>What's that mean? It means the openings are not placed the same way. An owl compares when sound reaches each ear and how loud it is.</v>

144
00:15:19.109 --> 00:15:29.705
<v Professor Hoot>Those timing and loudness clues help locate left and right, or above and below. Two ears, one very accurate map. A fact, properly charted.</v>

145
00:15:30.055 --> 00:15:34.975
<v Old Timber>A useful chart, Professor. And a true fact worth hearing from both sides.</v>

146
00:15:40.827 --> 00:15:47.507
<v Old Timber>Then Splish, Splash, and Splosh, the three Mudpuppy brothers, splashed in for Mudpuppy Minutes.</v>

147
00:15:47.857 --> 00:15:52.867
<v Splosh>Today on Mudpuppy Minutes: why does your own voice sound different when you hear a recording?</v>

148
00:15:53.217 --> 00:15:56.068
<v Splish>The recording removes your secret speaking hat.</v>

149
00:15:56.418 --> 00:16:03.538
<v Splash>Incorrect! The tiny person inside the recorder does an impression of you, but with less lunch.</v>

150
00:16:03.888 --> 00:16:14.623
<v Splosh>Both wrong. When you speak, you hear sound through the air and vibrations traveling through your head. A recording mostly gives you the air part.</v>

151
00:16:14.973 --> 00:16:18.251
<v Splish>Oh dear. That sounded dangerously answer-shaped.</v>

152
00:16:18.601 --> 00:16:21.279
<v Splash>Quick, put the speaking hat back on it!</v>

153
00:16:21.629 --> 00:16:26.709
<v Splosh>Too late. The recorder person has eaten lunch and grown stronger.</v>

154
00:16:27.059 --> 00:16:28.800
<v Splish>What did the tiny person eat?</v>

155
00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:33.318
<v Splash>A very small sandwich and the crunchy corner of a song.</v>

156
00:16:33.668 --> 00:16:41.060
<v Splosh>Mudpuppies, mudpuppies, we have solved it. Recordings sound different because lunch improves impressions.</v>

157
00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:44.330
<v Splish>And because heads are full of secret singing tunnels.</v>

158
00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:46.540
<v Splash>Tunnels need tickets.</v>

159
00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:48.450
<v Splosh>Charge the sandwich.</v>

160
00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:52.840
<v Splash>The sandwich accepts puddle coins and one loud coupon.</v>

161
00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:59.935
<v Old Timber>Your voice sounds different on a recording because you normally hear it through the air and through vibrations inside your head.</v>

162
00:17:00.285 --> 00:17:09.045
<v Old Timber>A recording mainly captures the sound that traveled through the air. No speaking hat, tiny performer, ticket, or sandwich is required.</v>

163
00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:22.430
<v Old Timber>Today, you might notice one small thing you do not understand. Ask one careful question. Try one honest test. Let the answer be ordinary if it is ordinary, and let the wonder stay.</v>

164
00:17:22.780 --> 00:17:32.260
<v Old Timber>And remember: an ordinary sound is not a private message from God. Careful searching honors truth; it does not turn a plunk into a promise.</v>

165
00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:37.883
<v Rex>My official listening face is ready for serious questions and roots that surprise us.</v>

166
00:17:38.233 --> 00:17:43.793
<v Old Timber>Until another little sound brings us close, this is Old Timber at Shiloh Creek.</v>

167
00:17:44.143 --> 00:17:47.506
<v Ranger Ray>See you next time, somewhere in Shiloh Creek.</v>
